<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Burma</h1><p class="st-meta">1943 CE – 1945 CE · capital Rangoon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Japanese-sponsored Burmese state of 1943 to 1945, led by Ba Maw, whose army under Aung San eventually turned against Japan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-burma">British Burma</a> in 1942 with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a>, formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades">Thirty Comrades</a>. On 1 August 1943 Japan granted nominal independence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a>, a pre-war premier, became head of state, or Adipadi, of the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a>, which declared war on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a> governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> with a cabinet including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> as war minister and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">Thakin Nu</a> as foreign minister, but the Japanese army controlled the economy and military affairs. Forced labour, notably on the Burma-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> Railway, requisitioning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> brought hardship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> handed two eastern Shan states, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kengtung-state">Kengtung</a> and Mongpan, to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disillusioned nationalists formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-peoples-freedom-league">Anti-Fascist Organisation</a>, and in March 1945 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a> National Army rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. British forces retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> in May 1945, ending the state; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a> fled to Japan. Its leaders went on to negotiate independence in 1948.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-burma.jpg" alt="State of Burma" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Jaume Ollé /; derivative work by Rannpháirtí anaithnid · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burma_1943.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1943 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rangoon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ba Maw</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burmese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burmese State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=state-of-burma" aria-label="State of Burma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7296 1552 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Burma lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7616 1808L7616 1809M7616 1808L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7616 1809h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7616 1808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7616 1808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=state-of-burma">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
